\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Cost of the monotone constraint (increments forced non-negative, minus signed), $T=1024$, paired by seed, $n=12$. Torus = paper task (trained at $T=128$); recency = return the $k$-th most recent symbol past uncounted filler (trained at $T=1024$). The MapWM torus row is the stored sign ablation (six-arm loss-matching pool); all other rows are the pre-registered MONOTONE batch. Raw = difference in accuracy; loss-matched = compared at equal final training loss by pooled regression; unmeasured is not evidence of zero. Reading: for the two WM-type generators the cost is large on the map task and small on the counting task, while single-origin MapEM pays more than MapWM on recency, a difference that is unmeasured.\relax }}{6}{table.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:monotone}{{4}{6}{Cost of the monotone constraint (increments forced non-negative, minus signed), $T=1024$, paired by seed, $n=12$. Torus = paper task (trained at $T=128$); recency = return the $k$-th most recent symbol past uncounted filler (trained at $T=1024$). The MapWM torus row is the stored sign ablation (six-arm loss-matching pool); all other rows are the pre-registered MONOTONE batch. Raw = difference in accuracy; loss-matched = compared at equal final training loss by pooled regression; unmeasured is not evidence of zero. Reading: for the two WM-type generators the cost is large on the map task and small on the counting task, while single-origin MapEM pays more than MapWM on recency, a difference that is unmeasured.\relax }{table.caption.16}{}}
